# `libperfmgr` Power Profiles

In lieu of comments, this document is presented.

## Configs

We have three `powerhint.${sku}.json` configs, parsed by `libperfmgr` and chosen on init via `vendor.powerhal.config` (see `init.nx.rc`).

The `odin` config handles T210 performance, based on `CPU_PLL_CVB_TABLE_ODN` and `NA_FREQ_CVB_TABLE`.

The `modin` config handles performance for `modin` and `fric` which have more robuse cooling and power solutions than `vali` and can support the range of T210B01 freqs, based on `CPUB01_PLL_CVB_TABLE` and `GPUB01_NA_CVB_TABLE`. We rely on `cpufreq` and `devfreq` closest freq matching for the SLT freqs instead of having a billion configs for different tables.

The `vali` config is tuned for stripped-down B01 performance due to its limited nature.

## Clocks

Clocks are based on DVFS tables for ODN (`odin`) and B01. Defaults are chosen based on HOS (low-power and standard profiles) and Shield (perf mode, now mapped to `FIXED_PERFORMANCE` hint fired by `devicesettings`).

Overclocking is more complicated, we will likely just set max freqs to infinite on `FIXED_PERFORMANCE` or `SUSTAINED_PERFORMANCE` and allow the OC sysfs node to handle whether OC happens or not. This does not allow for fine-grained control, so we may opt to set up some other system for setting `userspace` governor or something like that and allowing users with experience to manually set freqs.

## `nvcpl`, `nvphs`

`libperfmgr` replaces both here. It does not do all CPL does but this is for the best, especially on `nx` where undefined behavior tuned for Shield tables is nsuboptimal. We do not implement CPL's app profiles functionality or some of PHS's hinting--this is stubbed in `tegra-common` and not much uses these APIs anyway.
